// 'Contemporary Bathroom' — Mareck (CC0, public domain; crediting the
// artist encouraged; original at blendswap.com/blends/view/75302). A
// tiled bathroom lit through a single window plane — mirror, gold
// fixtures, and a foam-lipped tub. Official pbrt-v4 export from
// https://benedikt-bitterli.me/resources/ — `scenes/corpus/fetch.sh
// bathroom` materializes the source; this RON was bootstrapped once by
// `cenote-cli import sources/bitterli/bathroom/scene-v4.pbrt` and is
// the source of truth — re-imports diff into scratch, never over it.
// Native oracle: pbrt path, maxdepth 17.
//
// Import fidelity: two knowing gaps, one louder than it sounds. The
// Floor and Foam displacement (bump) textures are dropped
// (material-depth work, unassigned) — and the window grazes both
// surfaces, the lighting angle where bump maps darken hardest, so the
// flat import renders the tub foam ~1.9× and the tile floor ~3×
// brighter than pbrt; strip displacement from the pbrt reference and
// the foam agrees to 2%. The rug's cutout silhouette — a shape
// `alpha` texture, a gap when this landed — imports since the bistro
// rung landed shape-alpha, and is curated onto the Rug material's
// geometry_opacity below (a small-in-frame effect either way). What
// remains after both is a ~10% room-wide brightness surplus, the same
// Schlick-vs-exact-Fresnel model class veach-ajar documented. The ×10
// anisotropic-roughness warnings are all equal-axis — averaging is
// exact, not a gap; texture `filter` hints are cosmetic.
(
    version: 2,
    ops: [
        Settings((
            name: "main",
            resolution: Some((1024, 1024)),
            spp: Some(64),
            max_bounces: Some(17),
            seed: None,
        )),
